\chapter{The Operators Feature: Predicate Composition}
\label{ch:operators}

\section{Why conditions should be values}

A rule engine, a validator, a rewriter, a lexer: most of the machinery of a
domain-specific language is a pile of conditions. ``Accept the token if it
is an identifier and it is not a keyword.'' ``Rewrite the node if it is a
binary application whose left child is a literal.'' ``Reject the input if
the span is empty and the offset is zero.'' Written directly, each of these
is a lambda that is immediately called, and the conditions cannot be
combined, stored, tested, or reused.

The operators feature exists to make a condition a \emph{value}. It
provides a wrapper, \api{dsl::Predicate}, that holds a boolean-returning
callable and offers three ways to build larger conditions from smaller
ones: conjunction, disjunction and negation. Once a condition is a value,
it can be stored in a container, returned from a function, captured by a
lambda, or combined with others into a single larger condition. This is the
difference between a rule engine that has to be rewritten when the rules
change shape and one that does not.

\section{The data model}

The feature occupies two small pieces of \texttt{DSLtk.hpp}: the
\code{Predicate} template at lines 6000--6036 and the factory
\code{dsl::predicate} at lines 6044--6049. The template is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename F> struct Predicate
{
  F fn;
  explicit constexpr Predicate (F f) : fn (std::move (f)) {}
};
\end{lstlisting}

The design is minimal to the point of austerity, and each of the three
choices in that snippet earns its place.

The callable is stored \emph{by value}, in a public member named
\code{fn}. Storing by value means a predicate owns its condition, so it can
be copied, moved, stored in a container and returned from a function without
any lifetime subtleties. Making the member public is a pragmatic choice:
the wrapper is a thin veneer, and a user who needs to reach the underlying
callable --- to inspect a captured value, say --- can do so without a
friend declaration or an accessor.

The constructor is \code{explicit}, so a predicate is never produced by an
accidental conversion from a callable. This matters because
\code{Predicate} participates in overload resolution through its member
operators: an implicit converting constructor would make almost any
callable look like a predicate and would produce surprises in code that
combines predicates with other things.

The constructor is \code{constexpr}, which means a predicate can be built
during constant evaluation. A \code{static constexpr} member of a DSL class
can therefore be a predicate, and the compiler can fold the whole thing
away.

The factory is

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename F>
constexpr auto
predicate (F &&f)
{
  return Predicate<std::decay_t<F>>{ std::forward<F> (f) };
}
\end{lstlisting}

As in the pipeline feature, the decay is what makes the type of a predicate
depend on what it computes rather than on how it was spelled. A predicate
built from a lambda lvalue, a lambda rvalue or a function reference all have
the same type, and therefore compose with each other.

The call operator forwards:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename T>
constexpr bool
operator() (T &&v) const
{
  return fn (std::forward<T> (v));
}
\end{lstlisting}

Because it takes a forwarding reference and forwards, a condition may
accept its argument by value, by const reference or by rvalue reference, and
the wrapper does not care which.

\section{The three operators}

\begin{notebox}[The operators live on the predicate]
\code{dsl::Operators::Mixin} defines \emph{no} operators. It adds a
single static factory, \code{make_pred}, and nothing else. The
conjunction, disjunction and negation are member operators of
\code{Predicate} itself, so they are available to every predicate whether
or not the user has inherited the feature tag.
\end{notebox}

The three operators are:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename G>
constexpr auto
operator& (Predicate<G> other) const
{
  return Predicate{ [a = fn, b = other.fn] (auto &&v)
                      { return a (v) && b (v); } };
}

template <typename G>
constexpr auto
operator| (Predicate<G> other) const
{
  return Predicate{ [a = fn, b = other.fn] (auto &&v)
                      { return a (v) || b (v); } };
}

constexpr auto
operator!() const
{
  return Predicate{ [a = fn] (auto &&v) { return !a (v); } };
}
\end{lstlisting}

Each operator returns a \emph{new} predicate. The composition is
eagerly described but lazily executed: the composed predicate stores the
two operands and evaluates them only when it is called. The conjunction
operator captures the left callable as \code{a} and the right as \code{b},
and the body is \code{a(v) && b(v)}; because \code{&&} is the built-in
short-circuiting operator, the right condition is not evaluated at all when
the left one is false. The same holds for the disjunction operator and
\code{||}. The negation operator captures a single callable and applies the
built-in \code{!}.

\begin{property}[Short-circuiting]
The composed predicate evaluates its left operand first and evaluates its
right operand only if the left operand did not already determine the result.
The number of evaluations of the underlying conditions is therefore a
function of the value being tested, not only of the shape of the
composition.
\end{property}

The three operators use the built-in \cpp{} spellings, and in \cpp{} those
spellings are not bound to \code{bool}. An overloaded \code{operator!} may
return any type, so a class can define a negation that yields a class
rather than a truth value. \DSLtk{}'s \code{Predicate} does exactly that:
\code{!p} is a predicate, not a \code{bool}. This is worth contrasting with
the D port, where the situation is reversed. In D, the \code{!} operator
always produces a \code{bool}, whatever the operand's type, so a D
predicate cannot be negated with \code{!}. The D port therefore uses
\code{~ p} --- the bitwise complement operator, which
preserves the operand's type --- and additionally provides a
\code{negated()} method. \DSLtk{}'s C++ \code{Predicate} has neither
\code{negated()} nor any other named negation: \code{!} is the only
negation, and it works because \cpp{} lets a class define what \code{!}
means for it.

The C++ \code{Predicate} also has no \code{operator bool}. A predicate
cannot be used directly in an \code{if} condition; it must be called. That
is a deliberate restriction, and it has a good reason: an implicit
conversion to \code{bool} would be a narrowing conversion that a reader
could not see, and the value of the predicate is a function of the argument
it is applied to, not a property of the predicate itself.

\section{What the feature tag adds}

The tag \api{dsl::Operators} (lines 6143--6158) has a mixin containing
one static member function:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename F>
static constexpr auto
make_pred (F &&f)
{
  return dsl::predicate (std::forward<F> (f));
}
\end{lstlisting}

\code{make_pred} is a thin, named wrapper over the free function
\code{dsl::predicate}. Its purpose is discoverability: a user who has
inherited \code{dsl::Operators} can find the factory by looking at the
class, and a DSL can present the factory as part of its own vocabulary.
The \DSLtk{} header's own comment shows the intended use:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
struct MyDSL : dsl::DSL<MyDSL, dsl::Operators> {};

auto rule = MyDSL::make_pred ([] (int x) { return x > 0; })
          & MyDSL::make_pred ([] (int x) { return x < 100; });
\end{lstlisting}

The last line does not compile as the header is shipped; the section that
follows explains why and what to do about it.

\section{A defect in the shipped composition operators}

\begin{warnbox}[The shipped operators do not compose out of the box]
As shipped, \code{p & q}, \code{p | q} and \code{!p} fail to compile when
\code{Predicate}'s callable is a closure type. The reason is a
class-template-argument-deduction subtlety in the three operator bodies,
explained below. The workaround is to erase the callable to a type the
composed lambda converts to, most simply \code{std::function<bool(int)>}.
\end{warnbox}

Each of the three operators ends with a return statement of the form

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
return Predicate{ [a = fn, b = other.fn] (auto &&v) { ... } };
\end{lstlisting}

The author clearly intended class template argument deduction to deduce
\code{Predicate} from the braced lambda. It does not. Inside the class
template \code{Predicate}, the name \code{Predicate} is the
\emph{injected-class-name} of the current instantiation, which denotes
\code{Predicate<F>} --- the instantiation in which the operator is being
defined --- and not the primary template. A braced-init-list naming a
class that is not a template-name is therefore not subject to deduction at
all: the compiler tries to construct a \code{Predicate<F>} from the
composed lambda, and fails, because a closure type is not convertible to
\code{F}.

The diagnosis is confirmed by the diagnostic itself, which reports
``no matching function for call to
\code{Predicate<F>::Predicate(<brace-enclosed initializer list>)}'' rather
than anything about deduction.

The operators are not entirely unusable. They compile exactly when the
composed lambda is convertible to \code{F}, because in that case the
construction \code{Predicate<F>(lambda)} succeeds and the composed callable
is stored, type-erased, in the \code{fn} member. A user who wants
composition has two options.

The first is to erase the callable to a type-erased function. This is the
approach taken by the listing below:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
using IntPredicate = dsl::Predicate<std::function<bool (int)>>;
\end{lstlisting}

A captureless lambda converts to \code{std::function<bool(int)>}, and so
does the composed lambda, so the construction succeeds. The cost is that
each predicate now holds a \code{std::function}, which may allocate.

The second is to define a small callable wrapper of one's own --- one with
a template constructor that accepts any callable --- and to instantiate
\code{Predicate} with that wrapper. The wrapper is then the erased type,
and the same reasoning applies.

The remaining sections describe the intended semantics, which are exactly
those of the source, and which the two workarounds deliver.

\section{Composition trees and evaluation}

The three operators build a tree. \code{p & q | !r} is grouped by the
built-in precedence rules, in which \code{&} binds more tightly than
\code{|}, so the expression is \code{(p & q) | (!r)}. Figure~\ref{fig:pred-tree}
shows the resulting tree.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\begin{dslgraph}
    \node[dslsmall] (root) {\code{p} \& \code{q} \code{|} \code{!r}};
    \node[dslsmall, below left=of root] (andn) {and};
    \node[dslsmall, below right=of root] (notn) {not};
    \node[dslsmall, below=of andn] (p) {\code{p}};
    \node[dslsmall, right=of p] (q) {\code{q}};
    \node[dslsmall, below=of notn] (r) {\code{r}};
    \draw[dsledge] (root) -- (andn);
    \draw[dsledge] (root) -- (notn);
    \draw[dsledge] (andn) -- (p);
    \draw[dsledge] (andn) -- (q);
    \draw[dsledge] (notn) -- (r);
\end{dslgraph}
  \caption{The composition tree of \code{p} \& \code{q} \code{|} \code{!r}.
  The root is a disjunction; its left child is a conjunction of the leaves
  \code{p} and \code{q}, and its right child is the negation of the leaf
  \code{r}.}
  \label{fig:pred-tree}
\end{figure}

The evaluation of such a tree is the obvious one, and
The pseudocode block that follows states it in a form that makes the
short-circuiting explicit.

\begin{pseudocode}[Evaluating a composed predicate]
\textbf{function} \code{eval}(node, v)\\
\quad \textbf{if} node is a leaf f \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{f(v)}\\
\quad \textbf{if} node is a conjunction (a, b) \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} \code{eval(a, v)} is false \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{return} false\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{eval(b, v)}\\
\quad \textbf{if} node is a disjunction (a, b) \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} \code{eval(a, v)} is true \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{return} true\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{eval(b, v)}\\
\quad \textbf{if} node is a negation a \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} not \code{eval(a, v)}
\end{pseudocode}

Because the composed predicate is a single closure, the tree is not walked
recursively at run time; the nesting is realised in the structure of the
closure's call operator. A conjunction whose left child is itself a
conjunction becomes a closure that calls another closure, and the call
stack grows with the depth of the tree. This is the price of the
representation, and it is small: the depth of a hand-written rule set is
rarely more than a handful.

\section{The cost of composition}

Each composition allocates a \emph{new closure type}. The result of
\code{p & q} is a \code{Predicate} whose callable is a fresh lambda type
that captures a copy of \code{p}'s callable and a copy of \code{q}'s
callable. The result of \code{(p & q) & r} is a third fresh type that
captures a copy of the composed closure --- which itself contains copies of
\code{p} and \code{q} --- and a copy of \code{r}'s callable.

Two consequences follow. The first is that the storage of a composed
predicate grows linearly in the number of leaves, because each level
copies its children. The second is that the composed predicate's \emph{type}
is unique: there is no way to write a function that accepts
``any composition of \code{p} and \code{q}'' without erasing the type, which
is precisely why the workaround in the listing below erases to
\code{std::function}.

Note also that composition is left-deep for a chain of conjunctions.
\code{a & b & c} groups as \code{(a & b) & c}, so the intermediate
composition is copied into the next level. A right-deep arrangement would
copy less, but the language's associativity rules decide this, not the
user.

\section{Predicates as values}

Because a predicate is an ordinary object, it can be stored. This is the
property that distinguishes the feature from a bag of lambdas, and it is
what makes a rule table possible:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::function<bool (int)>>> rules;
rules.emplace_back ("positive", RuleDSL::positive);
rules.emplace_back ("even", RuleDSL::even);
\end{lstlisting}

A predicate can equally be returned from a function, captured by a lambda,
or stored in a member of a DSL class. The type of the stored object is
whatever the erasure target is; with a fixed callable type the vector
would hold a vector of \code{Predicate<F>} and would be homogeneous, which
is often exactly what a rule table wants.

\section{Predicates and the match table}

The \chapref{ch:pattern-match} facility dispatches on a \emph{key}. It is
natural to ask whether a predicate can serve as a match guard, that is, as
the key of a \code{dsl::when<>} clause. The answer in the shipped code is
no, and the reason is worth recording.

\code{dsl::when<Key, F>} takes its key as a non-type template parameter, so
the key must be a constant expression of a structural type. A
\code{constexpr} predicate does satisfy those requirements, and
\code{dsl::when<p>(handler)} compiles. But the clause never matches.
\code{WhenClause::try_invoke} knows exactly two ways to recognise a key:
if the key's type has a static \code{matches} member taking a
\code{std::string_view}, that member is called; otherwise the key is
compared with \code{operator==} against the clause's key. A predicate has
neither a static \code{matches} member nor an \code{operator==}, so the
comparison branch is not even instantiated, \code{try_invoke} returns
\code{false}, and the dispatch falls through to the next clause --- and
ultimately to \code{otherwise}, or to the runtime error if there is none.

So the two facilities are disjoint. A predicate is a value that is applied
to an argument; a match key is a constant that is compared against. A user
who wants a guard-like behaviour must either use a \code{dsl::pattern<>}
key, which the match table does understand, or apply the predicate to the
dispatched value inside the handler.

\section{Filters and transformations}

A predicate is a \emph{filter}: it answers a yes-or-no question about a
value and produces nothing. A transformation produces a new value from an
old one, and the pipeline feature of \chapref{ch:pipeline} is the
corresponding tool.

The distinction is worth keeping sharp, because the two compose along
different axes. A pipeline is a chain: each stage consumes the previous
stage's output, and the chain has a definite order. A predicate
composition is a tree: the leaves are independent conditions about the same
argument, and the tree has no order of application beyond the precedence
rules.

The practical difference shows up when the question is ``which of these
conditions holds?''. A pipeline answers that by running stages until one
fails. A predicate tree answers it by combining the conditions with
disjunction, which evaluates every leaf that is not short-circuited away.
For a rule engine that must report \emph{all} the reasons a value was
rejected, a disjunction of predicates is the wrong tool: it stops at the
first true leaf. A vector of predicates, each evaluated independently, is
the right one.

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:operators-complete} builds a small rule DSL, prints a
truth table over an integer domain, and stores the rules in a container.
Because of the defect described above, the composed predicates use the
type-erased \code{IntPredicate} alias, while the single predicates use the
factory directly.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp, caption={A complete program that composes
predicates over an integer domain and prints the truth table. The composed
predicates use a type-erased callable so that the shipped composition
operators can be used.},
label={lst:operators-complete}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <functional>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The feature tag contributes only a named factory.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
struct RuleDSL : dsl::DSL<RuleDSL, dsl::Operators>
{
  static constexpr auto positive
      = dsl::predicate ([] (int x) { return x > 0; });
  static constexpr auto even
      = dsl::predicate ([] (int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A type-erased predicate.  The composed operators build a new Predicate
//  from a composed lambda, so the callable type must be one that the
//  composed lambda converts to.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
using IntPredicate = dsl::Predicate<std::function<bool (int)>>;

int
main ()
{
  //  Single predicates need no erasure.
  auto positive = RuleDSL::positive;
  auto even = RuleDSL::even;
  auto via_mixin = RuleDSL::make_pred ([] (int x) { return x < 10; });

  std::cout << "positive(4)  = " << positive (4) << '\n';
  std::cout << "even(4)      = " << even (4) << '\n';
  std::cout << "via_mixin(4) = " << via_mixin (4) << '\n';

  //  Composed predicates.
  IntPredicate p { std::function<bool (int)> ([] (int x) { return x > 0; }) };
  IntPredicate q { std::function<bool (int)> ([] (int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }) };

  auto both = p & q;
  auto either = p | q;
  auto not_p = !p;

  std::cout << "\n  x    p    q   p&q  p|q  !p\n";
  for (int x = -3; x <= 3; ++x)
    {
      std::cout << " " << x << "    " << (p (x) ? 1 : 0) << "    "
                << (q (x) ? 1 : 0) << "    " << (both (x) ? 1 : 0) << "    "
                << (either (x) ? 1 : 0) << "    " << (not_p (x) ? 1 : 0)
                << '\n';
    }

  //  Predicates are ordinary values: they can be stored.
  std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::function<bool (int)>>> rules;
  rules.emplace_back ("positive", positive);
  rules.emplace_back ("even", even);
  for (auto const &[name, pred] : rules)
    std::cout << name << "(4) = " << pred (4) << '\n';
}
\end{lstlisting}

The program prints

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
positive(4)  = 1
even(4)      = 1
via_mixin(4) = 1

  x    p    q   p&q  p|q  !p
 -3    0    0    0    0    1
 -2    0    1    0    1    1
 -1    0    0    0    0    1
 0    0    1    0    1    1
 1    1    0    0    1    0
 2    1    1    1    1    0
 3    1    0    0    1    0
positive(4) = 1
even(4) = 1
\end{lstlisting}

