\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{There is no \code{operator bool}.} A predicate cannot be
used directly as a condition; it must be called. There is also no
\code{operator==}, so two predicates cannot be compared, and a predicate
cannot be a key of an associative container without an additional wrapper.

\item \textbf{There is no \code{negated()} method.} In the C++ port,
\code{!} is the only negation, and it works because \cpp{} allows a class
to define what \code{!} means. The D port, whose \code{!} always yields a
\code{bool}, must use \code{~} or \code{negated()} instead.

\item \textbf{Composition requires an erasure or a workaround.} As
described above, the shipped operator bodies do not compile with a closure
type as the callable. The composition operators work with a type-erased
callable, or with a user-defined callable wrapper.

\item \textbf{Composition nests.} Each operator returns a new object whose
callable contains copies of its operands' callables. A composition of
$n$ leaves therefore stores $n$ callable copies, and a left-deep chain of
$n$ leaves has call depth $n$. Deeply nested rule sets should be
flattened by hand.

\item \textbf{Precedence follows the built-in rules.} \code{&} binds
tighter than \code{|}, and both are left-associative. A user who intends
\code{p & (q | r)} must parenthesise, because
\code{p & q | r} means \code{(p & q) | r}. Parentheses are cheap;
use them.

\item \textbf{There is no disjunction of a predicate with a raw
callable.} Both operands of \code{&} and \code{|} must be predicates. A
user who wants to mix in a bare lambda must wrap it with
\code{dsl::predicate} first.

\item \textbf{Short-circuiting can surprise.} Because the right condition
is not evaluated when the left one decides the result, a condition with a
side effect --- a predicate that logs, say --- will not be called in every
case. Predicates with side effects are a bad idea in any case, but the
short-circuiting makes the surprise concrete.

\item \textbf{No integration with the match table.} A predicate cannot be
used as the key of a \code{dsl::when<>} clause; see the discussion above.

\item \textbf{No integration with the rewrite rules.} The rewrite feature
of \chapref{ch:rewrite} has its own rule representation. A predicate may be
used inside a rewrite's guard by capturing it in the rule's lambda, but it
is not a first-class part of the rule syntax.

\item \textbf{The D port differs in spelling.} In D, a single
\code{opBinary} member handles both binary operators and chooses between
them by comparing the operator string at run time; the negation uses
\code{opUnary("~")}. The C++ port uses three separate member overloads,
selected at compile time, so the run-time comparison of the operator name
has no C++ counterpart.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The operators feature turns a condition into a value and gives that value
three ways to grow. Its design is right in almost every particular --- the
callable is owned, the constructor is explicit and constexpr, the
composition is lazy and short-circuiting --- and the one defect, the
inability to compose closure-typed predicates out of the box, is a
localised problem with a documented workaround.

The next chapter moves from values to dispatch. \chapref{ch:pattern-match}
introduces \api{dsl::match}, \api{dsl::when} and \api{dsl::otherwise}, the
facility that selects a handler by comparing a runtime key against a table
of compile-time keys.
